have very good development on the King  side so that's how eventually you're  going to play with this Bishop your  Bishop here on the c8 square you're  eventually going to want to thrust this  pawn on the B file and you're gonna want  to get the Bishop on this long diagonal  aimed right at the Kings side so few key  concepts to look at at the very  beginning we're gonna go continue  forward with the mainline white thrust  for trying to gain center control with  pawn to d4 and black here is just going  to  sure this with pawn takes d4 obviously  black wants to take away Center control  white now has two options he can take  with the knight take with the queen  obviously you don't want to take with  the Queen early on with white just  because you don't want to get your queen  involved too early and if you do take  black can always develop his knight here  with c6 and black will have to retreat  his queen losing a tempo so more than  likely you will see Knight captures d4  again these moves that I'm saying should  not be done if they are done by your  opponent you should definitely take  advantage of those moves and exploit  your your mistakes by your opponents  so from here black usually continues  with Knight to f6 basically putting  pressure on this  efore square trying to put more pressure  on the center black or excuse me white  now has to respond to defend this the  best way to do that is the main line  which is just simply Knight to c3 and  now there are three very good variations  that we're going to touch on and later  videos we will go a little more in-depth  the first one is the NASS tour which is  just pawn to a6 and this is actually a  very aggressive move white can no longer  come with his bishop and attack the the  King  eventually you can thrust forward with  this pawn to  b5 it has a defender from the a6 square  your Bishop can come on this long  diagonal attack so at the outset it  doesn't look like it does much but as  the game goes forward it's going to be a  very good move as you try to attack the  King side the second move that we're  going to look at is simply the classical  variation which is just a development of  the knight here to c6 white can always  capture here and then black can take on  c6 and begin to thrust forward again for  center control so that is the classical  and then last we have the dragon a lot  of times you will see people try to  fianchetto on the King side they'll  bring their Bishop to g7 Castle kingside  and build up a defense that way so a lot  of different variations that you can do  it this is a very good defense it's a  very attacking defense you can get a lot  of good play from it so those are some  of the key concepts to keep in mind  later videos we will go into some of the  other variations thanks again for  watching hope you enjoyed hope you've  learned a lot definitely check out the  other videos check out the website and  be sure to subscribe so you know when  new videos are coming out thanks for  watching

